      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "For very-low-birth-weight (VLBW) infants, breastfeeding may no longer be an immediate option. Mothers often turn to mechanically expressing their milk with a breast pump to provide their infants human milk. Research aim: This study aimed to describe mothers' experiences expressing milk for their VLBW infants in a level 3 neonatal intensive care unit."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Qualitative, phenomenological methods were used to better understand the milk expression experiences of 17 mothers of VLBW infants. In-depth interviews were audio-recorded and transcribed verbatim. Transcripts were analyzed using Colaizzi's seven-step protocol, and themes were identified to illustrate the lived experience of the mothers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Five global themes emerged from interviews with mothers. However, this article focuses on one global theme, \"I had one job and that was to make milk,\" and the supporting subthemes: (1) \"I was heartbroken,\" (2) \"Pumping is a full-time thing,\" and (3) \"I literally sacrificed nights.\""
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This study provides insight into the complex experience that mothers in the southeastern United States undergo when expressing milk for their VLBW infants."
        }
      ]},
